    "case_id": "amb1-case02-nonprofit-scale",
    "focal_region": {
      "annotated_sentence_text": "[Turn 1 USER]\ne001\tI direct a small nonprofit that runs evening digital-literacy workshops for adults.\ne002\tA community center across town, Willow Hub, wants us to start a second site in autumn.\ne003\tTheir neighborhood has very little free support, and our mission is access.\ne004\tBut our current site is inconsistent: some nights we have thirty participants, some nights twelve, and volunteer coverage is fragile.\ne005\tI cannot tell whether expansion would force us to become better or expose that the first site is not actually stable.\n[Turn 1 ASSISTANT]\ne006\tExpansion will not automatically create discipline; it can just duplicate uncertainty.\ne007\tBefore committing, I would separate demand, delivery capacity, and what you mean by a successful site.\ne008\tWillow Hub's interest establishes a possible channel, not yet reliable attendance or staffing.\ne009\tIf the current variation comes from scheduling or outreach, a second location might help.\ne010\tIf it comes from an unclear program or exhausted volunteers, another site could magnify it.\n[Turn 2 USER]\ne011\tYou are treating the current variation as a warning sign, but some of it is normal.\ne012\tParticipants work shifts, care for relatives, and come when they can.\ne013\tThe people who attend repeatedly have learned a lot—we have stories of people applying for jobs and managing appointments online.\ne014\tI do not want “stable attendance” to become a standard that excludes the reality of the people we serve.\n[Turn 2 ASSISTANT]\ne015\tThat is an important correction.\ne016\tConsistency in this setting may need to describe the program's availability and learning quality, not the same people attending every week.\ne017\tThe question could be whether participants can enter irregularly and still receive coherent help, and whether volunteers can deliver that without improvising everything.\ne018\tExpansion might serve the mission even with variable attendance, provided the operating model is designed for variability rather than judged against a classroom ideal.\n[Turn 3 USER]\ne019\tWillow Hub can give us a room and promote the sessions.\ne020\tI had been talking as if they would also coordinate locally, but their director clarified that they cannot promise staff time.\ne021\tOur volunteer lead still wants to do it.\ne022\tShe says new volunteers are more likely to join something that feels like growth than something described as fixing operations.\ne023\tI partly agree, and I partly think we are using inspiration to avoid the unglamorous work.\n[Turn 3 ASSISTANT]\ne024\tThe room and promotion are meaningful, but local coordination was carrying more of your imagined model than you realized.\ne025\tVolunteer excitement can be a source of capacity, yet it is risky to count people before they have committed to specific shifts and responsibilities.\ne026\tYou could test that assumption directly: recruit for named roles at the second site before announcing a launch.\ne027\tIf the energy is real, it should translate into some concrete ownership, though not necessarily all the ownership you need.",
